<header class="aw-head">
<h1>Artwork of the Day</h1>
<p class="aw-lede">Works are grouped by teaching week, one for each class meeting. Click an image for the full entry on that work.</p>
</header>
<section class="aw-week" id="week-1">
<header class="aw-week-head"><strong>Week 1</strong><span>September 15–17</span></header>
<div class="aw-week-grid"><article class="aw text-work" id="w1">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-09-15">Tuesday, September 15</time><span>Geography and prehistory</span></p>
<h3><i>關雎</i><span>Guanju</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><a class="poem-art" lang="zh-Hant" href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/14zBq2lNosY1tXPEup8yw-8dO-dBbtf85vsd17JJPF2k/edit" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Read the full text of Guanju"><span>關關雎鳩，在河之洲。</span><span>窈窕淑女，君子好逑。</span><span>參差荇菜，左右流之。</span><span>窈窕淑女，寤寐求之。</span><span>求之不得，寤寐思服。</span><span>悠哉悠哉，輾轉反側。</span><span>參差荇菜，左右采之。</span><span>窈窕淑女，琴瑟友之。</span><span>參差荇菜，左右芼之。</span><span>窈窕淑女，鐘鼓樂之。</span></a></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">Shijing (Classic of Poetry), Mao no. 1</p>
<p class="src">Text: Shijing (Classic of Poetry), Mao no. 1. Received text in ten couplets.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/14zBq2lNosY1tXPEup8yw-8dO-dBbtf85vsd17JJPF2k/edit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Guanju · course handout</a></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">Who is in it, where, and what happens?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">Four stanzas, one plant, three verbs. What does the repetition do?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">How does a love song become a lesson in statecraft?</span></li></ol>
</div></article>
<article class="aw portrait" id="w2">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-09-17">Thursday, September 17</time><span>Archaeology and writing</span></p>
<h3><i>三星堆青銅大立人</i><span>Standing figure</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="http://www.art-and-archaeology.com/china/sanxingdui/sx02.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/02-sanxingdui.jpg" width="1200" height="1800" alt="Standing figure" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">Sanxingdui, Guanghan, Sichuan</p>
<p class="src">Bronze, c. 1200 BCE. Figure 1.8 m; total height 2.62 m with base; c. 180 kg. Excavated 1986, Sanxingdui sacrificial pit 2. Sanxingdui Museum.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="http://www.art-and-archaeology.com/china/sanxingdui/sx02.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Large Standing Figure · art-and-archaeology</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_Colossal_Standing_Figure,_Sanxingdui_a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · CC0</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">Describe it without naming it. Hands, eyes, feet, scale.</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">Compare those hands to a Shang ritual vessel. What is each object for?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">Shang left records; Sanxingdui did not. What does that do to the history?</span></li></ol>
</div></article></div>
</section>
<section class="aw-week" id="week-2">
<header class="aw-week-head"><strong>Week 2</strong><span>September 22–24</span></header>
<div class="aw-week-grid"><article class="aw portrait" id="w3">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-09-22">Tuesday, September 22</time><span>Mandate of Heaven, House of Ru</span></p>
<h3><i>大盂鼎</i><span>Da Yu ding</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Da_Yu_ding" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/03-dayuding.jpg" width="1460" height="1800" alt="Da Yu ding" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">Western Zhou ritual cauldron</p>
<p class="src">Bronze, reign of King Kang, c. 11th century BCE. Inscription of 291 characters. Excavated Qishan, Shaanxi, 19th century. National Museum of China, Beijing.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Da_Yu_ding" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Da Yu ding · Wikipedia</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Da_Yu_ding.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · CC BY-SA 4.0</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">Where is the writing, and who could ever see it?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">It cooks offerings for the dead. How does that change the claim?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">Who is a Mandate claim addressed to?</span></li></ol>
</div></article>
<article class="aw" id="w4">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-09-24">Thursday, September 24</time><span>House of Mo challenges the Ru</span></p>
<h3><i>曾侯乙編鐘</i><span>Bells of Marquis Yi of Zeng</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bianzhong_of_Marquis_Yi_of_Zeng" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/04-zengbells.jpg" width="1600" height="1063" alt="Bells of Marquis Yi of Zeng" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">Sixty-five bronze bells from one tomb</p>
<p class="src">Bronze, buried 433 BCE. Excavated 1978, Leigudun, Suizhou, Hubei. Hubei Provincial Museum.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bianzhong_of_Marquis_Yi_of_Zeng" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bianzhong of Marquis Yi · Wikipedia</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bianzhong_of_Marquis_Yi_of_Zeng.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · CC BY-SA 2.0</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">Count what you see. What did it cost to make?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">Each bell sounds two pitches. What knowledge is behind that?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">Does the object prove Mozi right or prove him a philistine?</span></li></ol>
</div></article></div>
</section>
<section class="aw-week" id="week-3">
<header class="aw-week-head"><strong>Week 3</strong><span>September 29–October 1</span></header>
<div class="aw-week-grid"><article class="aw portrait" id="w5">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-09-29">Tuesday, September 29</time><span>House of Dao; Ru 2.0</span></p>
<h3><i>人物御龍帛畫</i><span>Man Riding a Dragon</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silk_painting_depicting_a_man_riding_a_dragon" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/05-dragonrider.jpg" width="1350" height="1800" alt="Man Riding a Dragon" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">Painted silk banner from a Chu tomb</p>
<p class="src">Ink and colour on silk, Warring States period, 475–221 BCE. 37.5 × 28 cm. Excavated 1973, Zidanku tomb 1, Changsha. Hunan Museum.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silk_painting_depicting_a_man_riding_a_dragon" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Man riding a dragon · Wikipedia</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Figure_Driving_a_Dragon_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · Public domain</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">What is the man doing, and where is he going?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">It was folded into a coffin. What is a painting for if its viewer is dead?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">Riding the six vapours: metaphor, claim, or instructions?</span></li></ol>
</div></article>
<article class="aw" id="w6">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-10-01">Thursday, October 1</time><span>Ru 3.0 and the House of Fa</span></p>
<h3><i>商鞅方升</i><span>Shang Yang&#x27;s measure</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://govt.chinadaily.com.cn/s/202109/10/WS613b0c99498e6a12c12057ea/ancient-rectangular-measuring-vessel-set-standard-for-measuring.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/06-shangyang.jpg" width="1800" height="1200" alt="Shang Yang&#x27;s measure" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">The standard volume of the state of Qin</p>
<p class="src">Bronze, 344 BCE (18th year of Duke Xiao of Qin), inscribed with Shang Yang&#x27;s name. Length 18.7 cm, capacity 202.15 cm³. Re-inscribed under the First Emperor. Shanghai Museum.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://govt.chinadaily.com.cn/s/202109/10/WS613b0c99498e6a12c12057ea/ancient-rectangular-measuring-vessel-set-standard-for-measuring.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The measuring vessel · China Daily</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_Shang_Yang_Measure_(9910503875).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · CC0</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">What is it, and what would you do with it?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">Why put the maker's name and the exact volume on the object?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">Is a standard measure fairness, extraction, or both?</span></li></ol>
</div></article></div>
</section>
<section class="aw-week" id="week-4">
<header class="aw-week-head"><strong>Week 4</strong><span>October 6–8</span></header>
<div class="aw-week-grid"><article class="aw portrait" id="w7">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-10-06">Tuesday, October 6</time><span>Qin: the First Emperor</span></p>
<h3><i>兵馬俑</i><span>Terracotta warrior</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://smarthistory.org/terracotta-army-emperor-qin-shi-huangdi/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/07-terracotta.jpg" width="1200" height="1800" alt="Terracotta warrior" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">Tomb complex of the First Emperor</p>
<p class="src">Earthenware, c. 210 BCE. Excavated from 1974, Lintong, Shaanxi. Museum of the Terracotta Army.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://smarthistory.org/terracotta-army-emperor-qin-shi-huangdi/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Terracotta Army · Smarthistory</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2011_Qin_Terracotta_Kneeling_Archer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · CC0</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">Look only at the face. What makes him a particular man?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">Moulded bodies, hand-finished heads. Why spend effort there?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">Is naming the maker good government or fear?</span></li></ol>
</div></article>
<article class="aw portrait" id="w8">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-10-08">Thursday, October 8</time><span>Han empire; Dong Zhongshu</span></p>
<h3><i>馬王堆帛畫</i><span>Funeral banner of Lady Dai</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://smarthistory.org/funeral-banner-of-lady-dai-xin-zhui/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/08-mawangdui.jpg" width="969" height="1800" alt="Funeral banner of Lady Dai" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">T-shaped painted silk, Mawangdui tomb 1</p>
<p class="src">Ink and colour on silk, 2nd century BCE. 205 × 92 × 47.7 cm. Excavated 1972, Mawangdui tomb 1, Changsha. Hunan Museum.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://smarthistory.org/funeral-banner-of-lady-dai-xin-zhui/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Funeral banner of Lady Dai · Smarthistory</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bani%C3%A8re_fun%C3%A9raire,_peinture_sur_soie,_Chine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · Public domain</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">Divide it into zones. What is in each?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">Find Lady Dai. How is she marked out?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">Does this illustrate correlative cosmology or do something else?</span></li></ol>
</div></article></div>
</section>
<section class="aw-week" id="week-5">
<header class="aw-week-head"><strong>Week 5</strong><span>October 13–15</span></header>
<div class="aw-week-grid"><article class="aw portrait" id="w9">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-10-13">Tuesday, October 13</time><span>The House of Fo enters</span></p>
<h3><i>金銅彌勒菩薩半跏思惟像</i><span>Pensive Bodhisattva</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilt-bronze_Maitreya_in_Meditation_(National_Treasure_No._83)" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/09-pensive.jpg" width="1080" height="1800" alt="Pensive Bodhisattva" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">Korean National Treasure no. 83</p>
<p class="src">Gilt bronze, Three Kingdoms period, late 6th–early 7th century. Height 93.5 cm. National Museum of Korea, Seoul.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilt-bronze_Maitreya_in_Meditation_(National_Treasure_No._83)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Treasure 83 · Wikipedia</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pensive_Bodhisattva_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · KOGL Type 1</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">Describe the pose. Weight, hand, eyes.</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">What is smooth and what is elaborate? Where did the effort go?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">What has to change for a state to adopt a foreign religion?</span></li></ol>
</div></article>
<article class="aw portrait" id="w10">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-10-15">Thursday, October 15</time><span>Sui, Tang, and Eurasia</span></p>
<h3><i>載樂駱駝俑</i><span>Camel with musicians</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://sogdians.si.edu/camel-with-musicians" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/10-tangcamel.jpg" width="1200" height="1800" alt="Camel with musicians" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">Sancai glazed tomb figure</p>
<p class="src">Sancai glazed earthenware, Tang dynasty, 8th century. A Bactrian camel carrying a troupe of musicians whose pointed caps indicate Sogdian performers.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://sogdians.si.edu/camel-with-musicians" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Camel with musicians · Smithsonian</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tang_Sancai_Porcelain_with_Musicians_on_a_Camel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · CC0</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">Count the figures. Faces, beards, hats, instruments.</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">Why put this in a tomb? What does it say about the dead?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">Openness, appetite, or empire? Can the object tell them apart?</span></li></ol>
</div></article></div>
</section>
<section class="aw-week single" id="week-6">
<header class="aw-week-head"><strong>Week 6</strong><span>October 20</span></header>
<div class="aw-week-grid"><article class="aw wide" id="w11">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-10-20">Tuesday, October 20</time><span>馬大王之清談 · The Great Debate</span></p>
<h3><i>步輦圖</i><span>Emperor Taizong Receives the Envoy</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_Taizong_Receiving_the_Tibetan_Envoy" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/11-bunian.jpg" width="1800" height="503" alt="Emperor Taizong Receives the Envoy" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">Attributed to Yan Liben</p>
<p class="src">Handscroll, ink and colour on silk. Attributed to Yan Liben (c. 600–673); the surviving scroll is generally taken to be a later Song copy. Palace Museum, Beijing.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_Taizong_Receiving_the_Tibetan_Envoy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bunian tu · Wikipedia</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buliantu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · Public domain</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">Who is seated, who stands, and how do you know the rank?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">The emperor is borne by women with fans and screens. What is that doing?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">Your house has one audience with the throne. What does this scene say about how to use it?</span></li></ol>
</div></article></div>
</section>
<section class="aw-week" id="week-7">
<header class="aw-week-head"><strong>Week 7</strong><span>October 27–29</span></header>
<div class="aw-week-grid"><article class="aw" id="w12">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-10-27">Tuesday, October 27</time><span>Song: cities, gender, Zhu Xi</span></p>
<h3><i>清明上河圖</i><span>Along the River During the Qingming Festival</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Along_the_River_During_the_Qingming_Festival" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/12-qingming.jpg" width="1800" height="900" alt="Along the River During the Qingming Festival" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">Zhang Zeduan · detail of the original scroll</p>
<p class="src">Handscroll, ink and colour on silk, Northern Song, early 12th century. 25.5 × 525 cm. Palace Museum, Beijing.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Along_the_River_During_the_Qingming_Festival" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Qingming shanghe tu · Wikipedia</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Along_the_River_During_the_Qingming_Festival_(detail_of_original).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · Public domain</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">Pick one square inch. Who is there and what do they do for a living?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">No frame, no vanishing point. How does a handscroll make you read a city?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">Zhu Xi wants granaries and family ritual. Is he describing this society or arguing with it?</span></li></ol>
</div></article>
<article class="aw wide" id="w13">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-10-29">Thursday, October 29</time><span>Heian court culture</span></p>
<h3><i>源氏物語絵巻</i><span>Genji Monogatari Emaki</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genji_Monogatari_Emaki" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/13-genji.jpg" width="1800" height="807" alt="Genji Monogatari Emaki" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">The Kashiwagi scene</p>
<p class="src">Handscroll, ink and colour on paper, 12th century. Japanese National Treasure. Tokugawa Art Museum, Nagoya, and Gotoh Museum, Tokyo.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genji_Monogatari_Emaki" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Genji Monogatari Emaki · Wikipedia</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Genji_emaki_Kashiwagi.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · Public domain</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">Where are you standing? What was removed so you could see in?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">If every face is the same, how does the painter show feeling?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">Does that make the Paulownia Court colder, or sharper?</span></li></ol>
</div></article></div>
</section>
<section class="aw-week" id="week-8">
<header class="aw-week-head"><strong>Week 8</strong><span>November 3–5</span></header>
<div class="aw-week-grid"><article class="aw portrait" id="w14">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-11-03">Tuesday, November 3</time><span>Final project workshop</span></p>
<h3><i>早春圖</i><span>Early Spring</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Early_Spring_(painting)" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/14-guoxi.jpg" width="1238" height="1800" alt="Early Spring" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">Guo Xi</p>
<p class="src">Hanging scroll, ink and light colour on silk, dated 1072. 158.3 × 108.1 cm. National Palace Museum, Taipei.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Early_Spring_(painting)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Early Spring · Wikipedia</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guo_Xi_-_Early_Spring_(large).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · Public domain</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">Trace a path from the bottom edge to the top. Where does it break?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">Find the three distances. What does each let you see?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">Does your project have a centre, or is it a list?</span></li></ol>
</div></article>
<article class="aw" id="w15">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-11-05">Thursday, November 5</time><span>The Mongol world; Goryeo</span></p>
<h3><i>八萬大藏經</i><span>Tripitaka Koreana</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tripitaka_Koreana" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/15-tripitaka.jpg" width="1357" height="1163" alt="Tripitaka Koreana" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">81,258 printing woodblocks</p>
<p class="src">Carved 1237–1248 during the Mongol invasions, replacing a set burned in 1232. Haeinsa, South Gyeongsang. UNESCO Memory of the World.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tripitaka_Koreana" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tripitaka Koreana · Wikipedia</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tripitaka_Koreana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · CC BY 2.0</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">What are you looking at, and how is it stored?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">Estimate the labour. Who feeds those people during an invasion?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">Religious act, military act, or a claim about who Goryeo is?</span></li></ol>
</div></article></div>
</section>
<section class="aw-week" id="week-9">
<header class="aw-week-head"><strong>Week 9</strong><span>November 10–12</span></header>
<div class="aw-week-grid"><article class="aw wide" id="w16">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-11-10">Tuesday, November 10</time><span>Rise of the samurai</span></p>
<h3><i>蒙古襲来絵詞</i><span>Illustrated Account of the Mongol Invasions</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M%C5%8Dko_Sh%C5%ABrai_Ekotoba" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/16-mokoshurai.jpg" width="1800" height="596" alt="Illustrated Account of the Mongol Invasions" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">Commissioned by Takezaki Suenaga</p>
<p class="src">Handscroll, ink and colour on paper, c. 1293. Records Suenaga&#x27;s service in the invasions of 1274 and 1281. Museum of the Imperial Collections, Tokyo.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M%C5%8Dko_Sh%C5%ABrai_Ekotoba" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mōko Shūrai Ekotoba · Wikipedia</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C5%8Dko_Sh%C5%ABrai_Ekotoba_Mongol_Invasion_Takezaki_Suenaga_2_Page_5-7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · Public domain</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">What happens in the scene, and where is Suenaga?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">He paid for it. Which details would he insist on?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">Code or claim: which one actually held Kamakura together?</span></li></ol>
</div></article>
<article class="aw wide" id="w17">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-11-12">Thursday, November 12</time><span>Yuan China; Muromachi Japan</span></p>
<h3><i>鵲華秋色圖</i><span>Autumn Colors on the Que and Hua Mountains</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://smarthistory.org/zhao-mengfu-autumn-colors-mountains/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/17-zhaomengfu.jpg" width="1800" height="548" alt="Autumn Colors on the Que and Hua Mountains" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">Zhao Mengfu</p>
<p class="src">Handscroll, ink and colour on paper, dated 1295. 28.4 × 93.2 cm. Painted for Zhou Mi. National Palace Museum, Taipei.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://smarthistory.org/zhao-mengfu-autumn-colors-mountains/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Smarthistory</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Autumn_Colors_on_the_Qiao_and_Hua_Mountains_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · Public domain</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">Find the two mountains. How does the brushwork differ?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">Painted from memory for someone who cannot go. What accuracy is it after?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">Can a landscape be an apology? What would you need to know?</span></li></ol>
</div></article></div>
</section>
<section class="aw-week" id="week-10">
<header class="aw-week-head"><strong>Week 10</strong><span>November 17–19</span></header>
<div class="aw-week-grid"><article class="aw dual" id="w18">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-11-17">Tuesday, November 17</time><span>Ming: overcoming the Mongols</span></p>
<h3><i>明太祖</i><span>Two portraits of the Hongwu Emperor</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hongwu_Emperor" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/18a-hongwu-plain.jpg" width="823" height="1230" alt="Two portraits of the Hongwu Emperor (1)" loading="lazy"></a><a class="shot" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hongwu_Emperor" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/18b-hongwu-court.jpg" width="609" height="1024" alt="Two portraits of the Hongwu Emperor (2)" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">The same founder, painted twice</p>
<p class="src">Left: the irregular or &#x27;unofficial&#x27; type, long-jawed and pocked. Right: the formal court portrait. Hanging scrolls, Ming dynasty and later.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hongwu_Emperor" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hongwu Emperor · Wikipedia</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Seated_Portrait_of_Ming_Emperor_Taizu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image 1</a> · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hongwu1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image 2</a> · Public domain</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">List the differences. Face, body, clothing, setting.</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">Who made each, for whom, to hang where?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">A peasant who became emperor is a problem. Does either portrait solve it?</span></li></ol>
</div></article>
<article class="aw" id="w19">
<header class="work-head">
<p class="when"><time datetime="2026-11-19">Thursday, November 19</time><span>Joseon: families and women</span></p>
<h3><i>초충도</i><span>Grass and Insects</span></h3>
</header>
<figure><div class="imgbox"><a class="shot" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shin_Saimdang#Paintings" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../images/artwork/19-sinsaimdang.jpg" width="1606" height="1800" alt="Grass and Insects" loading="lazy"></a></div></figure>
<div class="awt">
<p class="sub">Attributed to Sin Saimdang (1504–1551)</p>
<p class="src">Ink and colour on paper, first half of the 16th century. Poppy and lizard. Attribution traditional. Korea.</p>
<p class="out"><a class="more" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shin_Saimdang#Paintings" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sin Saimdang, paintings · Wikipedia</a><span class="imgsrc"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EC%8B%A0%EC%82%AC%EC%9E%84%EB%8B%B9_%EC%B4%88%EC%B6%A9%EB%8F%84(%E8%8D%89%E8%9F%B2%E5%9C%96)_%EC%96%91%EA%B7%80%EB%B9%84%EC%99%80_%EB%8F%84%EB%A7%88%EB%B1%80,_16%EC%84%B8%EA%B8%B0_%EC%A0%84%EB%B0%98.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">image source</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></p>
<ol class="q"><li><span class="lab">Elemental</span><span class="qt">What is growing, and what is crawling on it? Count the species.</span></li><li><span class="lab">Intermediate</span><span class="qt">A poppy and a lizard, at this scale. What is the subject?</span></li><li><span class="lab">Advanced</span><span class="qt">What separates the painter from the figure on the banknote?</span></li></ol>
</div></article></div>
</section>
<script>
(() => {
  if (!location.hash) return;
  const restoreAnchor = () => {
    const target = document.querySelector(location.hash);
    if (target) target.scrollIntoView({ block: 'start' });
  };
  const images = [...document.images];
  images.forEach(image => { image.loading = 'eager'; });
  addEventListener('load', restoreAnchor, { once: true });
  if (document.fonts) document.fonts.ready.then(restoreAnchor);
  Promise.all(images.map(image => image.decode().catch(() => {}))).then(restoreAnchor);
})();
</script>